    "passage": "If you have been to any banquet, the practice of toasting shouldn’t be foreign to you. But have you ever wondered when this became a tradition of celebration? In one version of the story, toasting dates back to ancient Romans, who clinked their glasses against one another’s. Meanwhile, the drinks would be [[1]] into other glasses. If there was anyone who wanted to poison others, this method could deter anyone with such an evil intention. [[2]], this assumption is not backed up with enough evidence. Another theory suggests that the act of toasting starts from the real toast itself. Dipping bread into wine may be hard to imagine now but was a pretty common practice in the Middle Ages [[3]] the poor quality of wine. Putting toast into a jar of wine was believed to [[4]] the wine ______ the unpleasant acidity and thereby improve the taste. Others hold that ancient Greeks drank to Gods as a ritual and for each other’s health. Cups filled with wine [[5]] to God as an exchange for one’s wishes, such as prosperity, love, or health. Whatever origin of toasting may have, this custom livens up our parties and banquets. Enjoy!",
